      <li><b>2026 is a proof year, not a revenue year.</b> ~=E2=82=AC54k ne=
t across five months of one-off studies. Their job isn't the cash =E2=80=94=
 it's converting the tobacco-meeting pilots and pet studies into subscripti=
ons. Don't judge the model on 2026.</li>
      <li><b>Q1 2027 is the inflection.</b> The moment paid pilots convert =
to chain subscriptions (JTI, PMI, then pet), recurring revenue starts compo=
unding and never resets. ARR goes =E2=82=AC0 =E2=86=92 =E2=82=AC421k net in=
 twelve months.</li>
      <li><b>The spikes are big one-off chain pilots</b> (Teta in Jun-27, C=
OOP in Aug-27). These are lumpy by nature =E2=80=94 a new chain's first pai=
d read lands as one package, then converts to a subscription a quarter late=
r. Cash-flow planning should expect this jaggedness, not a smooth ramp.</li=
>
      <li><b>This ledger reconciles to the annual model.</b> =E2=82=AC421k =
net exit ARR =E2=89=88 the smooth model's 2027 data figure =E2=80=94 the de=
al view and the per-location view agree, which is the point: the packages <=
i>are</i> the curve, just seen up close.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class=3D"mine"><b>Read this as shape, not gospel.</b> Deal names, =
sizes and months are modeled illustrations grounded in your pipeline and th=
e report's pricing =E2=80=94 the <i>pattern</i> (one-off proof in 2026 =E2=
=86=92 subscription compounding from Q1-27 =E2=86=92 lumpy chain pilots) is=
 the reliable part. Slip the tobacco-meeting conversion by a quarter and th=
e whole ledger shifts right by ~3 months.</div>
  </section>

